A compilation of previous STAR results in high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadron \(R_{\rm AA}\) measurements [67, 93, 104] is shown in Fig.2.7. With centrality differential measurements in these collision systems, we can extract a continuous picture on the evolution of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) modification as a function of system size. Most measurements agree with the intuitive trend that a bigger system equates to heavier jet modification. However, due to the limited statistics of earlier datasets, the uncertainties and number of bins presently available are not enough to depict a precise picture. Therefore, measurement in this thesis could greatly compliment this picture. Especially with the large statistics of the Ru+Ru/Zr+Zr collisions (see Sec.4.2), which can be done in much greater precision than previously achieved, we hope to improve the field's understanding on the evolution of jet quenching effect with the system size, and help finding the boundary where QGP effects start to dominate the modification.

