Although the embedding is performed in \(K^{\pm}\), \(\pi^{\pm}\) and \(p/\bar{p}\) separately, in actual data the spectrum is the sum of all species. Especially, the efficiency for \(K^{\pm}\) is lower than that for \(\pi^{\pm}\) and \(p/\bar{p}\). Therefore, we need to know the percentage each species occupies at each \(p_{\rm T}\) in order to properly construct the efficiency curve. STAR has previously measured the identified particle spectra in \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\,\)GeV \(p+p\) and \(d+\)Au collisions [94]. For \(p+\)Au collisions, I use both weightings to the process, use the \(d+\)Au value as nominal and quote their difference as uncertainty. The particle component for isobar collisions is in principle between that of \(d+\)Au collisions and Au+Au collisions. Previous research [147] has shown that they are comparable in the \(p_{\rm T}\) range of interest to this thesis. Therefore, for isobar collisions, I use the \(d+\)Au weighting as nominal value, and quote its difference with reconstruction done purely with pions only as uncertainty, since they have the highest abundance among the three.

